75, 0.05157470703125, -0.0162200927734375, 0.01837158203125, -0.00841522216796875, 0.01441192626953125, 0.0193634033203125, 0.01419830322265625, 0.035308837890625, -0.017578125, 0.03253173828125, 0.00954437255859375, 0.0333251953125, -0.01131439208984375, 0.005512237548828125, -0.003437042236328125, -0.019378662109375, 0.0089874267578125, -0.004528045654296875, -0.00702667236328125, 0.01358795166015625, 0.041534423828125, -0.01194000244140625, -0.01261138916015625, -0.0360107421875, 0.0239105224609375, -0.01309967041015625, -0.0011653900146484375, 0.035888671875, 0.0132904052734375, 0.0006594657897949219, -0.0158233642578125, -0.0027751922607421875, 0.00017273426055908203, -0.005260467529296875, -0.0228118896484375, 0.01788330078125, -0.034210205078125, -0.00878143310546875, -0.004364013671875, -0.0008616447448730469, 0.0200958251953125, 0.0091094970703125, -0.004299163818359375, -0.01044464111328125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines often optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, leading to fragmented supervision and limited transfer across generative tasks. For example, text-to-image (T2I) datasets may lack the editing annotations needed for image manipulation, while editing corpora are rarely aligned with structured knowledge sources. This siloed approach makes it challenging to systematically organize heterogeneous supervision and to leverage dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\nTo address these limitations, we introduce a \\textbf{capability-driven data infrastructure}: a unified framework that explicitly organizes and schedules data collection and curation according to the progression of model capabilities. This infrastructure is designed to support scalable, curriculum-aligned training for multimodal generative models. The framework is structured around three interoperable data engines, each responsible for a distinct type of \\textbf{capability-specific supervision}\u2014that is, tailored data and annotations for training models on specific generative tasks. We first describe these engines and then explain how they interact and support curriculum scheduling.\n\n(1) \\textit{Text-image grounding}: This engine pairs descriptive captions with images to enable T2I generation (e.g., matching \"a red sports car on a mountain road\" to its corresponding image). The focus here is on natural language descriptions that directly reference visual content, supporting the core T2I task.  \n(2) \\textit{Inter-image transformation}: This engine creates editing pairs by associating an original image with its modified version and the corresponding edit instruction (e.g., turning a daytime scene into nighttime based on a prompt). This supports tasks where the model must modify images according to textual instructions.  \n(3) \\textit{Image-knowledge association}: This engine links images to structured knowledge entities (e.g., connecting an image of the Eiffel Tower to its Wikidata entity). Unlike text-image grounding, which uses free-form captions, this engine provides connections to formal knowledge bases, enabling models to ground visual content in structured, external information.\n\nThe distinction between the engines is as follows: text-image grounding focuses on descriptive, natural language captions for direct T2I generation; inter-image transformation centers on pairs of images and edit instructions for manipulation tasks; and image-knowledge association connects images to structured, external knowledge entities, supporting knowledge-grounded generation and retrieval.\n\nThese engines are interoperable: for instance, an image and its caption from the grounding engine can serve as a base for generating editing pairs in the transformation engine, while knowledge associations can enrich both T2I and editing data with semantic context. For example, an image of a red sports car (with caption) can be linked to its car model entity in a knowledge base, and then used to generate an edited version (e.g., changing the car color) with an associated instruction. This interoperability enables the construction of multi-faceted datasets that support transfer and compositionality across tasks, with data flowing between engines to create richer supervision.\n\nTransitioning from the data engines, the curriculum scheduling process unfolds in a logically connected sequence designed to maximize model learning and transfer. The rationale for the specific order is to ensure that foundational capabilities are established before introducing more complex tasks, thereby improving model robustness and transferability.  \n- First, we introduce simpler capabilities (e.g., basic T2I grounding) before more complex ones (e.g., editing or knowledge association), ensuring foundational skills are established.  \n- Next, we adjust the visual-concept distribution to achieve broad and balanced coverage, building on the initial capability foundation.  \n- Then, we progressively enhance data quality and image resolution, leveraging the established concept diversity.  \n- Finally, we follow the dependency order of capability acquisition, so that advanced skills are layered atop robust foundational abilities. For example, editing tasks are introduced after the model demonstrates proficiency in T2I generation, ensuring a smooth progression and minimizing catastrophic forgetting.\n\nTo align T2I and editing supervision across tasks and granularities, we employ \\textbf{caption experts}: automated captioning models that are fine-tuned for accuracy and consistency using a held-out validation set and human-in-the-loop feedback. Caption experts are trained on diverse captioning datasets and further refined through periodic evaluation on benchmark tasks. Human annotators review a sample of generated captions at regular intervals, providing feedback that is used to update model parameters and filter low-quality outputs. Caption experts are selected based on their performance on benchmark captioning tasks and are periodically evaluated for coherence and relevance by expert annotators, ensuring high-quality supervision across the data engines.\n\nAt scale, our framework curates a 440M-image T2I corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs. For context, this surpasses the size and diversity of many existing datasets: for example, LAION-5B contains 400M English-language image-text pairs, while OpenImages offers 9M images with limited editing or knowledge annotations. To ensure data quality and diversity, we apply automated filtering for duplicates, inappropriate content, and annotation errors, as well as manual validation on sampled subsets. Our datasets exhibit higher diversity, as measured by unique object and scene categories, and broader coverage of compositional and knowledge-grounded scenarios.\n\nUsing this infrastructure, we train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at two scales, with 3B and 6B parameters respectively. For evaluation, we conduct quantitative assessment on CPI-Bench\u2014a comprehensive benchmark designed to test compositional, perceptual, and instruction-following abilities in generative models. CPI-Bench includes a diverse set of tasks that require models to combine multiple concepts, follow complex instructions, and demonstrate perceptual fidelity, offering a more rigorous and holistic evaluation than prior benchmarks. Metrics such as FID, CLIP score, and instruction adherence are used for quantitative analysis. Qualitative evaluations include reviews by a panel of 15 human and expert annotators, selected for domain expertise and diversity, with inter-rater reliability measured at 0.87 (Cohen\u2019s kappa) across diverse T2I and editing scenarios.\n\nExperimental results demonstrate broad visual coverage, versatile rendering, and effective transfer across generative capabilities. In summary, our capability-driven data infrastructure enables scalable, curriculum-aligned training for multimodal generative models, setting a new standard for dataset construction and model performance in large-scale image generation. Potential limitations include the computational cost of large-scale curation, the need for further refinement of capability dependencies, and the risk of biases or ethical concerns in data collection (e.g., representation gaps or inadvertent inclusion of sensitive content); future work will explore more efficient data selection, broader capability hierarchies, and enhanced ethical safeguards."}}